# ------------------------------------------------------------- take-up 6.3 --
old_tu = "Two things come out of the exercise. The first is that take-up decides the distributional sign, and the natural worry about these credits is correct. The ratio of higher- to lower-group participation is 1.40 at baseline. Claimed evenly, the credit compresses it to 1.19 at the Gift Aid rate and to 1.04 at the French rate: participation is a bounded quantity and the group with more room to move gains more, so a rebate everyone claims is equalising. Claimed unevenly, the arithmetic reverses. At half take-up the Gift Aid credit is distributionally neutral, 1.43 against 1.40, and at quarter take-up it is disequalising, 1.60; the French rate goes further, to 1.72, because the higher group is near saturation and absorbs the whole of the throttled inflow. The complementarity is what does it: the higher group's induced entry raises the belonging return for everyone, so the lower group is partly carried along, but not enough to offset a rebate it does not claim. A credit designed to build the social fabric can widen the participation gap it was meant to close, and whether it does turns on an implementation detail rather than on the rebate rate.\n\nSecond, and unaffected by the first, take-up disciplines the level without touching the aggregate sign. Gift Aid's aggregate falls from 66 to 56 percent as take-up drops to a quarter, and its cost falls from 3.2 to 2.0 percent of mean income, so the policy stays expansionary and stays cheap relative to the work subsidy throughout. The policy conclusion is therefore conditional in a way the earlier draft's was not: as an instrument for raising participation the credit dominates the work subsidy at every take-up rate examined, and as an instrument for narrowing the education gap it works only if it is claimed evenly."
new_tu = (
 f"Two things come out of the exercise, and the natural worry about these credits does not materialise here. The ratio of higher- to lower-group participation is {f2(ratio0)} at baseline. Claimed evenly, the credit compresses it to {f2(R['cred_25_100_hi']/R['cred_25_100_lo'])} at the Gift Aid rate and to {f2(R['cred_66_100_hi']/R['cred_66_100_lo'])} at the French rate: participation is a bounded quantity and the group with more room to move gains more, so a rebate everyone claims is equalising. Claimed unevenly, the credit equalises less but does not reverse: at half take-up the Gift Aid ratio is {f2(R['cred_25_50_hi']/R['cred_25_50_lo'])}, and at a quarter take-up it is {f2(R['cred_25_25_hi']/R['cred_25_25_lo'])}, still below the {f2(ratio0)} baseline; the French rate follows the same pattern, {f2(R['cred_66_50_hi']/R['cred_66_50_lo'])} at half take-up and {f2(R['cred_66_25_hi']/R['cred_66_25_lo'])} at a quarter. The complementarity is why it stays equalising even when claimed unevenly: the higher group's induced entry raises the belonging return for everyone, so the lower group is partly carried along by a rebate it does not itself claim.\n\n"
 f"Second, take-up disciplines the level without touching the sign or the ranking. Gift Aid's aggregate falls from {f1(pct(R['cred_25_100_r']))} to {f1(pct(R['cred_25_25_r']))} percent as take-up drops to a quarter, and its cost falls from {f1(R['cred_25_100_cost'])} to {f1(R['cred_25_25_cost'])} percent of mean income, so the policy stays expansionary and stays cheap relative to the work subsidy throughout. As an instrument for raising participation the credit dominates the work subsidy at every take-up rate examined; as an instrument for narrowing the education gap it does the same at every take-up rate examined, though less effectively as take-up becomes more uneven."
)
edits.append((old_tu, new_tu))

