old_gp1 = "The two ledgers rank the two fiscal policies in opposite orders. On material GDP the work subsidy wins outright, $+6.6$ percent against the Gift Aid credit's $-4.7$, since participation takes time that would otherwise be worked. On GDP-B the ranking inverts, $+-1.2$ against $+10.5$, and the France-rate credit widens the gap to $+13.5$. Empowerment raises both columns and costs nothing. A planner choosing on the first column picks the work subsidy; a planner choosing on the second picks the credit; and the two planners are looking at the same model, the same households and the same fiscal envelope (Figure \\ref{fig:gdpb})."
new_gp1 = (f"The two ledgers rank the two fiscal policies in opposite orders. On material GDP the work subsidy wins outright, $+{f1(R['gdp_work_subsidy_(20%)'])}$ percent against the Gift Aid credit's ${f1(R['gdp_participation_credit_(25%)'])}$, since participation takes time that would otherwise be worked. On GDP-B the ranking inverts: the subsidy is negative, ${f1(R['gdpb_work_subsidy_(20%)'])}$ percent, against the credit's $+{f1(R['gdpb_participation_credit_(25%)'])}$, and the France-rate credit widens the gap to $+{f1(R['gdpb_participation_credit_(66%)'])}$. Empowerment raises both columns and costs nothing. A planner choosing on the first column picks the work subsidy; a planner choosing on the second picks the credit, and at this calibration that second planner is not merely picking a smaller gain, since the subsidy actually lowers GDP-B (Figure \\ref{{fig:gdpb}}).")
edits.append((old_gp1, new_gp1))

old_gp2 = "The honest qualification is that the reversal here is one of ranking rather than of sign. At the model's own shadow price the work subsidy still raises GDP-B, by -1.2 percent: the fabric it destroys is worth less than the output it manufactures. It becomes GDP-B reducing only if a fully participating society's cohesion is worth more than 27 percent of GDP, where the model's own price is 33. An earlier draft of this paper reported the stronger claim, that the subsidy lowers GDP-B outright, and that claim was an artefact of the coarse numerical settings this version replaces. Which of the two statements is right in the world is not something this model can settle, because it turns entirely on the price of the fabric, which is exactly the quantity the WELLBY literature exists to estimate \\citep{layard2023}. That is the useful form of the result: the model supplies the quantities and the breakeven price, 27 percent of GDP per unit of participation, and the wellbeing-valuation literature supplies the number that decides the sign. What the model does settle on its own is the ranking, which does not depend on the price at all once the price is positive, since the credit dominates the subsidy on participation and the two differ by less than four points of GDP."
new_gp2 = (f"At the model's own shadow price the work subsidy lowers GDP-B, by {abs(R['gdpb_work_subsidy_(20%)']):.1f} percent: the fabric it destroys is worth more than the output it manufactures. That sign depends on the price, since the breakeven at which the two exactly offset is {f1(R['pi_star_pct'])} percent of GDP per unit of participation against the model's own {f1(R['pi_model_pct'])}, so the two are close and a reader who prices the fabric a little lower would find the subsidy GDP-B neutral rather than reducing. Which valuation is right in the world is not something this model can settle on its own, because it turns entirely on the price of the fabric, which is exactly the quantity the WELLBY literature exists to estimate \\citep{{layard2023}}. That is the useful form of the result: the model supplies the quantities and the breakeven price, and the wellbeing-valuation literature supplies the number that fixes how far from that breakeven the true price sits. What the model does settle on its own is the ranking, which does not depend on the price at all once the price is positive, since the credit dominates the subsidy on participation.")
edits.append((old_gp2, new_gp2))

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- 5.3 sec --
old_53 = "Third, the distance matters as much as the verdict, and it is a distance rather than a hair's breadth: the taste distribution would have to be compressed from a log standard deviation of $0.750$ to below $0.463$ on the analytical bound, or below $0.40$ on the model's own scan, before coordination traps became possible."
new_53 = (f"Third, the distance is modest, and stating it plainly is more useful than rounding it up: the taste distribution would have to be compressed from a log standard deviation of ${f3(R['sigma'])}$ to below ${f3(R['sigbar'])}$ on the analytical bound, or below ${f2(R['frontier'])}$ on the model's own scan, before coordination traps became possible, and Section 5.3 shows that margin narrows further at lower values of the private share $\\omega$.")
edits.append((old_53, new_53))

# --------------------------------------------------------------- conclusion --
old_con1 = "The paper's central result is a bound. In a discrete-choice model of social participation with lognormal tastes, multiple equilibria require taste dispersion below a threshold built from observed participation rates and one preference share, and that threshold tightens as the participation gradient steepens. Coordination traps and visible gradients are substitutes, and the data can be asked which it has. Asked of France, it answers gradients: the economy sits some sixty percent outside the coordination region on the analytical bound, and further still on the model's own numerical scan, which puts the fold at a dispersion of 0.40 against the 0.75 the data require. That is the verdict at the central private share, and the verdict itself holds at every private share examined; the margin behind it does not, which is why the private share is the quantity this framework most wants measured next. The result needs no structural estimation, so it travels to any setting where participation rates by group are known, which is most of them."
new_con1 = (f"The paper's central result is a bound. In a discrete-choice model of social participation with lognormal tastes, multiple equilibria require taste dispersion below a threshold built from observed participation rates and one preference share, and that threshold tightens as the participation gradient steepens. Coordination traps and visible gradients are substitutes, and the data can be asked which it has. Asked of France, it answers gradients, though not by a wide margin: the economy sits about ten percent outside the coordination region on the analytical bound, and a little further on the model's own numerical scan, which puts the fold at a dispersion of {f2(R['frontier'])} against the {f3(R['sigma'])} the data require. That is the verdict at the central private share, and the verdict itself holds at every private share examined between 0.15 and 0.50; the margin behind it varies substantially across that range and is thin at the low end, which is why the private share is the quantity this framework most wants measured next. The result needs no structural estimation, so it travels to any setting where participation rates by group are known, which is most of them.")
edits.append((old_con1, new_con1))

old_con2 = "The bound then does a second job, which is the part I did not anticipate when I set out to prove it. The slope it constrains is the same slope that sets the equilibrium multiplier on any policy, $1/(1-G')$, so a model disciplined by participation rates is a model whose amplification factor is no longer free. Here that factor is about two and a half: a financed work subsidy's 4.3-point direct effect becomes an 11-point fall, an empowerment policy that closes the agency gap moves participation by 2.5 points without a fiscal instrument, and a participation tax credit at Gift Aid's real rebate rate moves it by 31 points in the opposite direction at a sixth of the subsidy's fiscal cost. The same calibrated complementarity does all three pieces of arithmetic, and the theorem says why it must."
new_con2 = (f"The bound then does a second job, which is the part I did not anticipate when I set out to prove it. The slope it constrains is the same slope that sets the equilibrium multiplier on any policy, $1/(1-G')$, so a model disciplined by participation rates is a model whose amplification factor is no longer free; at this calibration the linear prediction is a factor of {f1(1/(1-R['slope0']))}, though the multiplier realised on the twenty-point shock examined is smaller once curvature is accounted for, {f1(R['amp'])}. A financed work subsidy's {f1(pct(r0-R['r_sub_direct']))}-point direct effect becomes a {f1(pct(r0-R['r_sub']))}-point fall, an empowerment policy that closes the agency gap moves participation by {f1(pct(R['r_emp']-r0))} points without a fiscal instrument, and a participation tax credit at Gift Aid's real rebate rate moves it by {f1(pct(R['cred_25_100_r']-r0))} points in the opposite direction at a fraction of the subsidy's fiscal cost. The same calibrated complementarity does all three pieces of arithmetic, and the theorem says why it must.")
edits.append((old_con2, new_con2))

old_con3 = "The policy conclusions are correspondingly conditional. A credit that pays for the act of joining outperforms a subsidy that pays for hours, on participation and on the inclusive ledger, at every take-up rate examined. Whether it also narrows the education gap depends on take-up, and at a quarter take-up it widens it. Whether the work subsidy is inclusive-output reducing rather than merely inclusive-output inferior depends on a price this model cannot supply: the fabric would have to be worth more than 27 percent of GDP, against the model's own 33 percent. Both are places where the framework hands a specific, answerable question to the wellbeing-measurement literature rather than answering it by assumption."
new_con3 = (f"The policy conclusions are correspondingly direct. A credit that pays for the act of joining outperforms a subsidy that pays for hours, on participation and on the inclusive ledger, at every take-up rate examined, and it narrows the education gap at every take-up rate examined too, though less so the more unevenly it is claimed. At the model's own price of the fabric, {f1(R['pi_model_pct'])} percent of GDP, the work subsidy is inclusive-output reducing rather than merely inclusive-output inferior, since that price exceeds the {f1(R['pi_star_pct'])} percent breakeven; a reader who values the fabric a little below the model's own estimate would find the subsidy merely GDP-B neutral. This is a place where the framework hands a specific, answerable question, the money value of participation, to the wellbeing-measurement literature rather than answering it by assumption.")
edits.append((old_con3, new_con3))

if __name__ == "__main__":
    tex = Path("paper/sage_sa.tex").read_text()
    missing = [o for o,_ in edits if o not in tex]
    changed = sum(1 for o,n in edits if o != n)
    print(f"{len(edits)} edits, {changed} change text, {len(missing)} NOT FOUND")
    for m in missing:
        print("   MISSING:", m[:140])
    if "--apply" in sys.argv and not missing:
        for o,n in edits: tex = tex.replace(o, n, 1)
        Path("paper/sage_sa.tex").write_text(tex)
        print("APPLIED")
